Benjamin Lewis wrote:

> Okay, perhaps I'm confused about something, but I was trying to figure out
> the chance of breaking a lance in a successful joust, and I engaged in some
> cursory source-diving.  To my surprise, it appears to me that the chance is
> minimized if luck is exactly 1.  The relevant code:
> 
>  1  if (joust_dieroll == 0 && rnl(50) == (50-1) &&
>  2      !unsolid(mon->data) && !obj_resists(obj, 0, 100))
>  3         return -1;      /* hit that breaks lance */
>  4 
>  5  int
>  6  rnl(x)          /* 0 <= rnl(x) < x; sometimes subtracting Luck */
>  7  register int x; /* good luck approaches 0, bad luck approaches (x-1) */
>  8  {
>  9     register int i;
> 10
> 11     i = RND(x);
> 12
> 13     if (Luck && rn2(50 - Luck)) {
> 14        i -= (x <= 15 && Luck >= -5 ? Luck/3 : Luck);
> 15        if (i < 0) i = 0;
> 16        else if (i >= x) i = x-1;
> 17     }
> 18
> 18     return i;
> 20  }
> 
> 
> Assume luck is > 0.
> 
> Line 1 will be true if rnl(50) is exactly 49.
> This will occur if:
>   - the RND(x) on line 11 returns 49 (1/50 chance)
>   - the rn2(50 - luck) call on line 13 returns 0 (1/(50-luck) chance)
> 
> Thus, the chance of breaking a lance is 1/(50*(50-luck)) for positive luck,
> which is minimized when luck == 1 (neglecting additional criteria on line
> 2).  (Actually, multiply that chance by 1/5 for the joust_dieroll, which is
> RND(5)).)
> 
> Is this the intent?  Am I misreading something?

You're quite right. Does not depend on the value of x but in general if 
luck is positive then propability of getting x-1 is:

1/(x*(50-luck))

which maximizes when luck == 1.

This cannot be the intent as the comment on rnl(x) clearly states that 
the propability of x-1 should be decrease when luck increases.

